WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.960
In the pursuit of greatness, we are asked to give our lives away.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.440
To relinquish our time.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.480
Our energy.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.640
And our bodies.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.360
All to reach the top of our chosen field.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.980
To become the greatest to have ever lived.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:20.940
To become a champion.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.040
"I waited four f*cking years for this day."

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.440
In Dota 2 that pinnacle is The International, The biggest esports tournament on the planet.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.480
"It all comes down to this."

00:00:30.700 --> 00:01:01.420
(Casting)

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:04.780
This is the story of teams risking it all for greatness.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:10.140
The story of people laying everything on the line for the privilege of hoisting the ever-elusive

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.960
Aegis of Champions in front of thousands of fans.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.700
This is the story of The International.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:27.340
The International may be the biggest tournament in esports today, but its beginnings were humble.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:33.480
In August of 2011, Valve hosted the first ever TI at Gamescom in Cologne as a coming

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:39.020
out party for Dota 2 – the sequel to popular Warcraft 3 mod, Defense of the Ancients.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:43.200
But the game, which was still in beta at the time, did not make a great first impression

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.200
with diehards.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:48.890
And at first, players were resistant to playing an updated version of a game that they had

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:50.680
poured their hearts and souls into.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:55.940
"Maybe one guy liked this thought it was pretty cool but, everybody was just like what the f*ck is this."

00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:00.140
"Initial reaction not just for myself it was just like this isn't Dota."

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:03.120
On-top of  grappling with an unfinished, buggy game,

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.680
the players didn't receive beta keys until a couple weeks out from the event.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:10.720
An event that boasted the largest cash prize esports had ever seen.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:15.660
Despite the bugs, how alien the game felt for everyone involved and many players showing

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:21.060
up with a relaxed attitude – one team, Natus Vincere showed up to win.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:26.900
"We were so confident, I remember we that were so confident we were going to win because I joined

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.440
in the game, and I feel so free."

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:34.840
Dendi was the face of Na’Vi and the Ukrainian mid laner more or less wrote the book on proactive

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.900
mid play, seizing any opportunity to change a game.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:42.420
And it was that aggressive approach that helped Na’Vi breeze through the event.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:47.160
Eventually Na'Vi would face Chinese powerhouse EHOME in the grand finals.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:51.879
And although the Chinese team would take a game off them – a feat no other team managed

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.640
throughout the tournament – Na’Vi would
prevail.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:07.840
(Casting)

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:12.800
But this clash between Europe and China would not be an isolated one, in fact it would set the

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:16.360
tone for Dota 2 and The International for years to come.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:23.400
By the time TI2 rolled around, Dota 2 had a full year of play under its belt and the

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.120
game had taken off in a big way, resulting in The International being moved to Benaroya

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.120
Hall in Seattle, Washington.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:35.620
But the storyline of TI2 centred around Na’Vi and whether China – the dominant region

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:41.720
in the competitive circuit – had truly closed the gap enough to dethrone the defending European champions.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.620
And during the group stage that appeared to be the case, as NaVi dropped more games than

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.300
LGD-Gaming, Invictus Gaming and Team DK combined.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:55.540
But in the second round of the upper bracket, Na'Vi ran into Group B winners Invictus Gaming.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:00.799
Despite having the odds stacked against them, Na’Vi reminded everyone why they had been

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.320
TI champions by producing one of the greatest plays in Dota 2 history.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.520
Simply known as “The Play.”

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:42.400
(Casting)

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:47.040
After sending Invictus Gaming down to the lower bracket, Na’Vi then managed to upset

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:52.770
an undefeated LGD Gaming in the upper-bracket finals, punching their ticket to yet another

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.310
International grand final.

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:58.550
“But NaVi for the third time in a row take down one of these top Chinese teams after

00:04:58.550 --> 00:04:59.540
losing game one.

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:02.300
They are proving to be the champions they were last year.”

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:07.080
There, they would rematch Invictus Gaming, who had valiantly clawed their way out of

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.800
a stacked lower bracket.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.760
With one million dollars and regional pride on the line, Europe and China clashed again

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:17.320
but this time Na’Vi was in over their head.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:06:09.800
(Casting)

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:14.979
China had exacted revenge for its previous TI defeat, and now, the rest of the world

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:18.380
would have to wait another year to knock them off their perch

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:28.680
With the tremendous success of TI2, the stakes for The International 3 had to be raised and

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:33.020
Valve’s introduction of the Compendium in 2013 did just that.

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:37.860
Now a TI staple, the Compendium allows Dota 2 players to help support The International.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:42.460
As an incentive to buy it, Valve implemented a tiered rewards system that gave players

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:45.640
in-game cosmetics each time a crowdfunding goal blew up.

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:50.220
And pledged to donate 25% of the money they received from compendium purchases toward

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:51.440
TI’s prize pool.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:55.840
And looking back, its release might be one of the most significant moments in not just

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:58.620
the history of Dota, but all of esports.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:04.380
From the get-go, the compendium was a hit, boosting TI3’s prize pool by more than 1.2

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:08.380
million dollars for a grand total of $2.87 million.

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:14.520
With the largest cash prize in esports history on the line, it was up to the teams to deliver the entertainment

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:15.800
And did they ever.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:08:34.480
(Casting)

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:39.980
A year after the Chinese Dota scene flexed their strength, the region dominated TI3’s bracket stage.

00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:43.380
And while Natus Vincere were in the running to make their third consecutive

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:48.300
TI grand final, there was another European team making waves on Dota’s biggest stage

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:49.360
Alliance.

00:08:49.360 --> 00:09:05.400
(Casting)

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:07.400
A  wholly Swedish roster that featured the likes

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:10.460
of Loda, s4 and AdmiralBulldog.

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:15.040
Alliance were one of the hottest teams coming into TI3 and were in the midst of one of the

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:17.860
most impressive seasons in Dota 2 history.

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:22.560
Having gone undefeated in the group stage, Alliance faced-off against Na’Vi in the

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:26.240
upper bracket and swept them down to the lower bracket.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:32.500
Once there, Na’Vi would face Orange Esports who had just upset both DK and TongFu in

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:34.000
back to back series.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:39.380
During game three, Orange was on the brink of upsetting NaVi as well, until KYXY committed

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:42.720
one of the costliest mistakes in Dota 2 history.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:43.600
The Deny.

00:09:43.720 --> 00:10:04.520
(Casting)

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:07.640
In a  now infamous, momentum shifting misplay that

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:12.580
remains hard to watch, the Malaysian Carry denied his team both the Aegis of Immortals

00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:15.520
and ultimately a grand finals berth.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:59.840
(Casting)

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:05.840
Orange’s disastrous exit paved the way for TI’s first ever all European Grand Final

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:10.280
and a rematch between Na’Vi and Alliance in what is widely considered to be the most

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:13.940
memorable grand finals series TI has ever seen.

00:11:13.940 --> 00:12:57.920
(Casting)

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:04.000
After trading games back and forth, with unique drafts, role swapping and heart-wrenching

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:08.740
plays — The International had its first ever five game Grand Finals.

00:13:08.980 --> 00:13:11.180
"It all comes down to this.

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:14.900
One more game, two-million dollars on the table.

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:20.000
Winner: first place at The International, the Aegis and the prestige of being the best

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:22.160
Dota 2 team in the world!’

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:29.320
And in the end, the thrilling final game it was all but decided by S4 and a pair of legendary dream coils

00:13:29.320 --> 00:14:55.860
(Casting)

00:14:55.900 --> 00:15:01.840
The Million Dollar Dream Coils etched S4’s name into Dota 2’s pantheon, cemented Alliance

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:07.069
as the undisputed best team in the world, and provided an incredible conclusion to what

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:11.800
remains the most exhilarating grand finals The International has ever seen.

00:15:18.760 --> 00:15:23.440
Prior to the beginning of The International 4, Valve and Dota fans started to get a taste

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:25.300
of what The Compendium was capable of.

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:30.550
By the time the tournament was about to begin, the community had raised over $9 million to

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:36.780
contribute to the prize pool making TI4’s $10.92 million the largest prize pool in esports history.

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:41.619
The format for the event had also changed, with the top two teams being awarded byes

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:45.960
ahead of the semifinals, in an attempt to make the group stage feel more significant.

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:50.040
With that said, The International 4 wasn’t exactly the most memorable TI.

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:54.120
And one of the reason for that was the “Death Ball” meta, which strongly rewarded teams

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:56.940
that executed aggressive early game tactics.

00:15:56.940 --> 00:16:01.100
Before the Deathball strategy’s discovery, the group stage had been pretty exciting as

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:03.700
teams tried to navigate their way through the new game build.

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:09.139
But, once Vici Gaming and Evil Geniuses uncovered it, all other battle plans were rendered obsolete.

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:13.860
Games during the elimination phase of the event were very one-sided and it often felt

00:16:13.869 --> 00:16:18.140
like whichever team was able to secure the lead early on would snowball and win, with

00:16:18.140 --> 00:16:19.900
little potential for comebacks.

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:24.820
And it was, in large part, thanks to the Deathball meta that North America would get to see its

00:16:24.829 --> 00:16:30.120
first deep run at an International title with Evil Geniuses looking like the best non-Chinese

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:31.540
team in the world.

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:43.600
(Casting)

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:49.160
But the North American TI dream would be short lived, as EG stumbled over Newbee in the upper

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:53.819
bracket and then Vici Gaming in the lower bracket finals, resulting in TI’s first

00:16:53.819 --> 00:16:55.999
ever all Chinese grand final.

00:16:55.999 --> 00:17:01.420
And it went down as one of the most anticlimactic grand finals of all time with Newbee perfectly

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:05.520
implementing the Death Ball strategy to easily dispatch their compatriots.

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:56.560
(Casting)

00:17:56.560 --> 00:18:01.760
While TI4 may have been tainted by its uninspired meta and lacklustre finale, its record-breaking

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:07.440
prize-pool raised mainstream awareness of the event and the level of prestige associated with it.

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:12.720
After the mixed reaction to TI4, Valve decided to return to the conventional

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:16.020
group and bracket stage formats of previous TIs.

00:18:16.020 --> 00:18:20.800
And that decision – combined with a less restrictive meta – would lead TI5 to being

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:23.540
considered one of the most competitive in the tournament’s history.

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:27.540
Heading into the event, Evil Geniuses were riding high after their deep run the previous

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:33.040
year, and since adding a 15-year old pub star named SumaiL in January, the team had emerged

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:35.600
as one of the non-Chinese favourites to win the event.

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:40.210
While the group stage was strongly contested, the bracket phase was still largely dominated

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:44.770
by the Chinese teams and even EG would find themselves falling down to the lower bracket

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:46.400
at the hands of CDEC.

00:18:46.400 --> 00:19:19.060
(Casting)

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:24.460
After their loss to CDEC, EG was staring down elimination, and their last chance at making

00:19:24.460 --> 00:19:29.920
grand finals relied on overcoming another giant of Chinese Dota in LGD Gaming.

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:30.960
And they did.

00:19:31.120 --> 00:20:16.720
(Casting)

00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:22.160
For the  second time in The International’s history, a grand final would be decided in an upper-bracket

00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:23.320
final rematch.

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:29.100
All that stood between EG and NA’s first TI championship was the imperious CDEC.

00:20:29.100 --> 00:20:34.660
After taking two of the first three games, EG looked to carry their momentum into the fourth.

00:20:34.660 --> 00:20:41.580
And nearly thirty minutes into that game, Evil Geniuses  emphatically slammed the door shut on CDEC and China’s

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:44.480
China’s hopes of claiming another TI title.

00:20:44.600 --> 00:21:10.760
(Casting)

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:15.560
Off the back of the $6 million dollar Echo Slam, perhaps the most iconic Dota 2 play

00:21:15.560 --> 00:21:19.000
in recent memory, Evil Geniuses closed out the match.

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:32.960
(Casting)

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:40.080
Universe Aui_2000, Fear, PPD, and SumaiL had done it – they had finally given NA its

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:45.680
first ever TI Championship, and they did so in the most spectacular of fashion.

00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:50.320
By now you may have noticed a trend with The International.

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:52.800
Every other year, China brings home the title.

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:56.880
That meant, that when it was time for The International in 2016, and its more than $20

00:21:56.880 --> 00:22:01.560
million prize pool, all eyes were on Dota’s most decorated region.

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:06.190
But standing in China’s way were defending champs Evil Geniuses, who looked primed to

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:08.000
make another run at the Aegis.

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:11.740
After the dust settled and the brackets were set, things looked just about how you’d expect

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:14.160
Including EG.

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:19.040
And in their second round upper bracket clash against EHOME, Evil Geniuses treated Dota

00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:21.960
2 fans to one of the greatest comebacks of all time.

00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:26.500
Finding themselves 20,000 gold down after the 40 minute mark and with EHOME securing

00:22:26.500 --> 00:22:32.200
megacreeps 30 minutes later, Evil Geniuses dug in and fought desperately for their tournament lives.

00:22:32.200 --> 00:24:00.780
(Casting)

00:24:00.780 --> 00:24:07.060
EG did what nobody had ever done before at a TI, coming back from mega creeps to keep their hopes

00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:09.480
of back-to-back titles alive.

00:24:09.480 --> 00:24:13.680
But despite their tremendous performance, NA’s defending champions bowed out of

00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:19.940
TI6 at the hands of China’s Wings Gaming and another NA team in the lower bracket Digital Chaos.

00:24:19.940 --> 00:25:14.840
(Casting)

00:25:14.840 --> 00:25:18.200
After losing to Wings earlier in the upper bracket, DC ran through a gauntlet

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:22.320
of tough opposition to earn their shot at redemption in the the grand finals.

00:25:22.360 --> 00:25:25.720
"This is the only team who could handle the lower bracket.

00:25:25.720 --> 00:25:27.120
And I don’t know how they did it.

00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:31.520
And they deserve so much credit, and I think they’ve just gotta do it now.

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:33.440
You can’t have this journey and not win.”

00:25:33.440 --> 00:25:39.179
Once again the largest prize-pool in esports history would be decided between North America

00:25:39.180 --> 00:25:39.920
and China.

00:25:39.920 --> 00:25:44.240
And while Digital Chaos fought courageously, Wings would not be denied.

00:25:44.320 --> 00:25:58.720
(Casting)

00:25:58.800 --> 00:26:04.480
Once again China raised the Aegis of Champions at the expense of NA and the rest of the world’s

00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:06.480
shattered TI dreams.

00:26:07.340 --> 00:26:12.140
The International is a tournament that is defined just as much by heartbreak as it is by

00:26:12.140 --> 00:26:17.200
historic victories and crazy plays — and no one embodied that heartbreak heading into

00:26:17.200 --> 00:26:19.080
TI7 more than Kuroky.

00:26:19.160 --> 00:26:25.060
He was always a celebrated name, but one haunted by a series of crushing disappointments.

00:26:25.120 --> 00:26:30.400
That trend of falling short looked to continue in 2017 when Liquid were eliminated from the

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:32.440
upper bracket in the first round.

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:54.120
(Casting)

00:26:54.680 --> 00:26:59.520
All eyes were on Liquid, who were once favoured to win the tournament, but now had one of

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:02.400
the longest roads to make it to the grand finals.

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:05.760
And under Kuroky’s leadership, they rallied.

00:27:05.760 --> 00:28:34.520
(Casting)

00:28:34.520 --> 00:28:39.780
After getting the best of Virtus Pro in an insane series, Liquid completed their unbelievable

00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:46.200
runback to grand finals by toppling LGD Gaming, then LGD Forever Young in the lower bracket finals.

00:28:46.200 --> 00:29:44.080
(Casting)

00:29:44.080 --> 00:29:46.320
And in grand finals, Liquid had just

00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:50.260
One dangerous obstacle between their captain and his dream, Newbee.

00:29:50.260 --> 00:29:53.280
But the Upper Bracket winners were simply no match.

00:29:53.280 --> 00:30:56.420
(Casting)

00:30:56.420 --> 00:31:02.380
After years of dedication, after years of painful disappointment – Kuroky’s sacrifice

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:04.320
was finally rewarded.

00:31:15.020 --> 00:31:20.400
The biggest stage, the most money and fans from all of the world screaming as you compete

00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:25.620
for the Aegis of Champions — The International is the shining star atop esports.

00:31:25.620 --> 00:31:30.040
TI is it, it’s do or die, make or break.

00:31:30.040 --> 00:31:34.200
When you retire from the game your whole career will be defined by how you perform

00:31:34.200 --> 00:31:37.620
at this event that takes place only once a year.

00:31:37.620 --> 00:31:42.740
As other esports have grown and spread their roots into corporate sponsorships and gained big investments

00:31:42.740 --> 00:31:45.960
through franchised systems, Dota has stood still.

00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:50.880
It is the last remaining relic of “the grind” – relinquishing your life to a game, to

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:54.280
realize its greatest reward or to come up short.

00:31:54.280 --> 00:32:00.060
TI is so enthralling that it defines a game, it breaks records and it breaks hearts.

00:32:00.160 --> 00:32:05.660
There’s no doubt that this pinnacle of gaming immortality offers a precarious means to defining

00:32:05.660 --> 00:32:06.620
your career by.

00:32:06.620 --> 00:32:08.700
But every year they come.

00:32:08.700 --> 00:32:13.940
And every year, dozens of stories are written into this event’s illustrious history.

00:32:13.940 --> 00:32:17.480
Because some things, are worth giving it your
all for.

00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:26.480
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

